note right of CallAudioDeviceModule
  Bridges libwebrtc's audio pipeline to Qt
  Multimedia — libwebrtc has no pluggable
  "push samples into an AudioSource" API,
  only this device-module interface (issue #46).

  Capture is push-style: CallManager (issue #70)
  forwards AudioInputDevice::pcmDataAvailable()
  straight into pushCapturedAudio() whenever a
  buffer is ready; forwarded to
  AudioTransport::RecordedDataIsAvailable()
  while StartRecording() is active. nBytesPerSample
  in that call (and NeedMorePlayData() below) means
  bytes per interleaved FRAME across all channels
  (sizeof(int16_t) * nChannels), not per scalar
  sample — WebRTC's own AudioTransportImpl asserts
  this and aborts (fatal CHECK) if it's wrong; this
  was a real bug here until issue #70 caught it live.

  Playout is pull-style but WebRTC does not
  drive the timing itself: this class runs its
  own ~10ms std::thread loop calling
  AudioTransport::NeedMorePlayData() and
  forwards decoded PCM to a caller-supplied
  PlayoutSink callback — invoked on that
  thread, NOT the Qt GUI thread. setPlayoutFormat()
  (issue #70) overrides the default 48kHz-mono
  request to match whatever real output device
  CallManager wired up, via AudioOutputDevice's
  streaming path; must be called before
  StartPlayout(). A caller touching Qt objects
  from the PlayoutSink callback must marshal back
  itself (e.g. QMetaObject::invokeMethod with
  Qt::QueuedConnection) — CallManager does.
end note

